1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,550
"Store below 30 °C" is printed on medicines across the hot half of the world.

2
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:12,948
It looks like advice. It is really a climate forecast — and it was made decades ago.

3
00:00:12,998 --> 00:00:16,329
This project checks whether that forecast still holds.

4
00:00:16,379 --> 00:00:23,264
Before a medicine is approved, batches sit in a test chamber for a year or more.

5
00:00:23,314 --> 00:00:31,004
WHO sets three chamber conditions: 25 °C for temperate markets, and 30 °C with 65% or 75% humidity for hot ones.

6
00:00:31,054 --> 00:00:38,073
Each setting was chosen to sit, with a safety margin, above the climate where the medicine will be stored.

7
00:00:38,123 --> 00:00:43,131
If the climate warms past it, every shelf life approved there becomes optimistic.

8
00:00:43,181 --> 00:00:50,736
The numbers come from 1971, when Haynes defined mean kinetic temperature — one number that captures heat damage.

9
00:00:50,786 --> 00:00:59,147
In 1998 Grimm set the 30 °C conditions with a margin over the climate of that time. In 2006 WHO added 75% humidity.

10
00:00:59,197 --> 00:01:07,223
The only recent re-check, for Japan in 2024, found no problem. A new global guideline, ICH Q1, is due in November 2026.

11
00:01:07,273 --> 00:01:12,615
The gap: we found no multi-decade, multi-country check of today's climate against these conditions.

12
00:01:12,665 --> 00:01:20,892
Our idea: treat the test condition as a hypothesis about the climate, and test it with the regulators' own method.

13
00:01:20,942 --> 00:01:27,290
Climate data become an effective temperature and humidity each year. We subtract them from the test condition.

14
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:34,360
What is left is the safety margin. Below zero, the climate has crossed the line, and shelf life shrinks.

15
00:01:34,410 --> 00:01:42,100
The formula is from 1971. What is new is repeating the audit every year, below country level, for heat and humidity.

16
00:01:42,150 --> 00:01:48,364
We analysed 18 places: 10 from ERA5 reanalysis and 8 from NOAA weather stations.

17
00:01:48,414 --> 00:01:54,427
We compared 1981–2000 with 2006–2025, and wrote our hypotheses and pass/fail rules before downloading any data.

18
00:01:54,477 --> 00:02:01,497
Three built-in checks: Tokyo as a positive control, a bias check against hourly station data, and reanalysis versus stations.

19
00:02:01,547 --> 00:02:06,889
Our daily method read 0.3 °C high, so every level you see is corrected.

20
00:02:06,939 --> 00:02:15,166
Result one: every one of the 18 places warmed, by 0.35 to 1.33 °C. Every 95% interval is above zero.

21
00:02:15,216 --> 00:02:20,223
Seven places now average above the test temperature their medicines are approved at.

22
00:02:20,273 --> 00:02:26,957
Tokyo, our control, stays safely inside Japan's 25 °C condition — exactly as the 2024 Japanese study found.

23
00:02:27,007 --> 00:02:33,892
Result two: inside the United States, which tests at 25 °C, two cities crossed the line.

24
00:02:33,942 --> 00:02:41,968
Houston rose from 24.68 to 25.41 °C, and from 5 to 16 years above 25 °C out of 20. Tampa crossed too.

25
00:02:42,018 --> 00:02:48,366
New Orleans now sits right on the line, above it in 9 of the last 20 years.

26
00:02:48,416 --> 00:02:55,436
We chose these as the warmest US cities, so this shows the problem exists, not how common it is.

27
00:02:55,486 --> 00:03:00,694
Result three: on humid coasts, water vapour breaks the chamber first.

28
00:03:00,744 --> 00:03:07,763
Chennai, Colombo and Yangon already hold more water vapour than the 30 °C, 65% chamber. Colombo exceeds 70% too.

29
00:03:07,813 --> 00:03:13,827
None yet reaches 75% — the reason WHO created that condition, now measured with current data.

30
00:03:13,877 --> 00:03:20,091
Why it matters: each extra degree speeds a typical degradation reaction by about 11%.

31
00:03:20,141 --> 00:03:26,825
So a 24-month label behaves like about 23 months in Houston, 20 in Dubai and 15 in Phoenix.

32
00:03:26,875 --> 00:03:34,900
This is outdoor climate, not measured product temperature — many buildings are cooled. But degraded antibiotics can under-dose patients and drive resistance.

33
00:03:34,950 --> 00:03:41,500
What changes: regulators can re-check test conditions every year with our open tool and data.

34
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:48,570
Places already over the line can be targeted for stability checks, and humid coasts get evidence for stricter humidity.

35
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:51,950
A standard is a forecast. Forecasts need checking.
